        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "853f84b5-86b7-5bf3-9280-e23a40c5c4cb",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Events: Battle of Gettysburg, Battle of Tarawa, Gettysburg Address cemetery consecration\n- Places: Gettysburg, Pennsylvania, Pacific Theater, Cemetery Ridge, Round Top, Little Round Top, Taneytown Road, Weikert Hill, Munshower Knoll, St. Francis Xavier Cemetery, Meade School\n- Systems: Pennsylvania Route 134, Round Top Branch, Hanover Junction, Hanover and Gettysburg Railroad\n- Topics: American Civil War, World War II, Pickett's Charge\n\nFacts:\n- The Battle of Gettysburg was fought on July 1–3, 1863, in and around Gettysburg, Pennsylvania, by Union and Confederate forces during the American Civil War.\n- In 1863, the road later designated Pennsylvania Route 134 was used for the procession to the cemetery consecration where the Gettysburg Address was delivered.\n- The Round Top Branch ran south from its junction with the Hanover Junction, Hanover and Gettysburg Railroad north of Meade School, west of Meade School and St. Francis Xavier Cemetery, across the field of Pickett's Charge, south of Cemetery Ridge, east of Weikert Hill and Munshower Knoll, and through Round Top to a point between the east base of Little Round Top and Taneytown Road.\n- The Battle of Tarawa was fought on November 20–23, 1943, in the Pacific Theater of World War II.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "9ee02fe5-c533-5bfd-a329-b94a69f3c939",
        "text": "Page: Battle of Gettysburg. Sentence: The Battle of Gettysburg ( , with an sound) was fought July 1–3, 1863, in and around the town of Gettysburg, Pennsylvania, by Union and Confederate forces during the American Civil War.\nPage: Battle of Tarawa. Sentence: The Battle of Tarawa was a battle in the Pacific Theater of World War II that was fought on 20–23 November 1943.\nPage: Pennsylvania Route 134. Sentence: The road was used during the 1863 Battle of Gettysburg for the procession to the cemetery consecration at which the Gettysburg Address was delivered.\nPage: Round Top Branch. Sentence: The branch ran southward from the terminus of the railroad's main line (its junction with the Hanover Junction, Hanover and Gettysburg Railroad north of Meade School), west of the school and St. Francis Xavier Cemetery, across the field of Pickett's Charge, south of Cemetery Ridge, east of Weikert Hill and Munshower Knoll, and through Round Top to a point between Little Round Top's east base and Taneytown Road.",
